E1       AXIS 1 -- THE AMBIENT GROUP.  xB017 worked in PSL(2,O_3).
         But the programme's own tower uses PGL(2,O_3) as the BASE (v_0 = covol PGL,
         the 1:12:24 tower).  ADDENDUM 3 found the deck group Z/2 IN PSL.  What is
         it in PGL?
         A FIRST TEST HERE WAS INCONCLUSIVE AND IS RECORDED: it conjugated by the
         single element diag(zeta_6,1) and found it does NOT normalise Gamma --
         but that is ONE REPRESENTATIVE of the coset, not the coset.  Corrected:
         coset classes tested: 12;  NORMALISING: 2 -> [8, 9]
         full check on the first hit, all 20480 elements: True
         => |N_PGL(Gamma)/Gamma| = 2 (PSL) + 2 (coset) = 4 = |Isom+(m004)| = 4
         AND THE THEOREM CONFIRMS IT INDEPENDENTLY: for an ARITHMETIC manifold
         Isom+(M) = N_{Comm(Gamma)}(Gamma)/Gamma, and the commensurator is the
         maximal group of the class -- PGL(2,O_3), since h = 1.
E1 PASS  ADDENDUM 3's Z/2 WAS ITSELF UNDER-STATED.  In the ambient
         group the programme actually uses, the deck group is Z/4 -- ALL of m004's
         orientation-preserving symmetry.

E2       AXIS 2 -- THE TORSION ORDER.  xB017 tested {2,3}: a PSL statement.
         in PGL, diag(zeta_6,1) acts as z -> zeta_6*z, of order 6.
         PSL sees the same cusp rotation as order 3 (it quotients by the units'
         squares); PGL sees ORDER 6.
           d =   1: |O^x| = 4  -> PGL cusp rotation of order 4   <- EXTRA
           d =   2: |O^x| = 2  -> PGL cusp rotation of order 2
           d =   3: |O^x| = 6  -> PGL cusp rotation of order 6   <- EXTRA
           d =   5: |O^x| = 2  -> PGL cusp rotation of order 2
           d =   7: |O^x| = 2  -> PGL cusp rotation of order 2
           d =  11: |O^x| = 2  -> PGL cusp rotation of order 2
           d =  15: |O^x| = 2  -> PGL cusp rotation of order 2
           d = 163: |O^x| = 2  -> PGL cusp rotation of order 2
         => only d = 1 (order 4) and d = 3 (order 6) carry extra PGL torsion, and
            ORDER 6 IS THE LARGEST TORSION ORDER OF ANY BIANCHI PGL GROUP.
E2 PASS  N2's 'orders {2,3}' is CORRECT AND PSL-SCOPED.  The
         programme's base orbifold is the PGL one, whose cusp torsion is Z/6 --
         a sharper d = 3 selector than anything xB017 measured.

E3       AXIS 3 -- THE OBJECT.  xB017 tested m004 only.  m003 IS THE A5 BIT.
         m004: PSL-index 12.0  |Isom| 8  |Isom+| 4  amphichiral True  H_1 Z
         m003: PSL-index 12.0  |Isom| 8  |Isom+| 4  amphichiral True  H_1 Z/5 + Z
         IDENTICAL on every invariant this arc used -- index, isometry group,
         amphichirality.  THEY DIFFER IN H_1 AND IN CONGRUENCE LEVEL: m003 at
         (2)^1 with quotient A_5, m004 at (2)^3 = (8)  (B734).
E3 PASS   Path B's machinery does NOT separate the sisters; the congruence LEVEL
         does.  That is the handle Path C needs, and it was sitting in this arc's
         own dependency (B734) unused.

E4       WHAT THE AUDIT CHANGES
         AXES xB017 VARIED: the field d (base rate), the torsion order within PSL,
           the cover level, the cover (full vs intermediate, via ADDENDUM 3).
         AXES IT DID NOT VARY, and what happened when this cell varied them:
           ambient group  -> deck group is Z/4 in PGL, not Z/2 (E1).  ADDENDUM 3
                             UNDER-STATED ITS OWN POSITIVE.
           torsion order  -> the base orbifold's cusp torsion is Z/6, not Z/3 (E2),
                             and 6 is the largest of any Bianchi PGL group.
           the object     -> m003 and m004 are indistinguishable by everything this
                             arc used; only H_1 and the congruence level separate
                             them (E3).
         PATH B's STANDING: BOTH torsion orders POSITIVE, now stronger -- the Z/2
           part is really Z/4 in the right ambient group, and the Z/3 part is
           really Z/6 there.  NEGATIVE only that the FULL index-24 (PGL) cover is
           regular.  The fences are unchanged: the cusp rotation does not descend
           to m004 (no order-3 or order-6 symmetry; |Isom+| = 4).
         AND THE METHOD LESSON, which is the owner's own definition of 'verify':
           THREE OF THIS ARC'S FOUR ERRORS WERE UNVARIED CONFIGURATIONS, NOT WRONG
           ARITHMETIC.  A negative is only as wide as the configuration space that
           was actually swept, and the sweep must be written down BEFORE the
